WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.760
Turn a newt's eye upside down, then cut the optic nerve and let it regrow.

2
00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:11.380
Hold a lure above its head, and the newt turns its head down.

3
00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:14.780
It kept making that mistake for as long as anyone watched.

4
00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:19.520
Sperry inferred that each regrown axon went back to the place it came from.

5
00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:21.720
How does an axon know where to go?

6
00:00:22.683 --> 00:00:26.603
The retina sends its axons to a target in the midbrain, the tectum.

7
00:00:27.063 --> 00:00:28.183
The map is orderly.

8
00:00:28.963 --> 00:00:31.603
Neighbors in the retina connect to neighbors in the tectum.

9
00:00:32.143 --> 00:00:37.043
In the chick, axons from the temporal retina, the side nearer the temple, end in the

10
00:00:37.043 --> 00:00:38.043
front of the tectum.

11
00:00:38.703 --> 00:00:40.363
Nasal axons end in the back.

12
00:00:41.317 --> 00:00:44.037
A growth cone is the moving tip of an axon.

13
00:00:44.497 --> 00:00:48.137
It senses only what is close, yet the map spans the whole target.

14
00:00:48.737 --> 00:00:50.637
The answer is a pair of opposite slopes.

15
00:00:51.377 --> 00:00:56.037
In the retina, the receptor EphA is high on the temporal side and low on the nasal

16
00:00:56.037 --> 00:01:01.037
side. In the tectum, the ligand ephrin-A rises from front to back.

17
00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:06.850
Temporal axons carry the most EphA, so a little ephrin-A repels them.

18
00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:09.450
Their endings form in the front of the tectum.

19
00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:14.830
Nasal axons carry little EphA and tolerate more ephrin, so their endings form

20
00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:21.150
farther back. In chick and mouse, axons first overshoot, then branch, and ephrin-A

21
00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:23.030
blocks branches behind the right zone.

22
00:01:24.083 --> 00:01:28.843
An axon might read an absolute level of EphA, or compare itself with its neighbours.

23
00:01:29.683 --> 00:01:33.923
In mouse, researchers gave a scattered half of the retinal ganglion cells extra

24
00:01:33.923 --> 00:01:38.583
EphA. If axons read absolute levels, only those cells should shift forward.

25
00:01:39.263 --> 00:01:44.023
Instead, with two copies of the knock-in, the cells with extra EphA took the front

26
00:01:44.023 --> 00:01:47.043
of the target, and their normal neighbours were pushed to the back.

27
00:01:47.043 --> 00:01:49.843
Two orderly maps filled the target together.

28
00:01:50.917 --> 00:01:53.517
So each axon is judged against the others.

29
00:01:54.117 --> 00:01:57.257
Its ending depends on its EphA signal relative to its neighbours.

30
00:01:58.137 --> 00:02:01.237
Axons compete for space, and the map is the outcome.

31
00:02:01.977 --> 00:02:04.637
Each growth cone senses only a short distance.

32
00:02:05.257 --> 00:02:09.897
Yet local rules give global order, partly because axons also sense each other.
